# Rank 1429 / CVE-2026-2293: NestJS's Fastify platform decides whether a
# middleware applies to a request by matching a path it derives itself, and it
# canonicalizes that path less than the router that dispatches the request does.
# A remote client respells the path and the middleware is skipped while the
# handler still runs.
#
# The weakness, in my own words. Fastify routes with find-my-way, whose match
# is taken over a normalized path: `ignoreDuplicateSlashes` collapses `//`
# runs, `ignoreTrailingSlash` drops a final `/`, `caseSensitive: false`
# lower-cases the path, `useSemicolonDelimiter` cuts it at the first `;`, and
# percent-decoding happens last. Nest reimplements middleware dispatch on top
# of that router in two places, and both applied only the decoding step:
#
#   packages/platform-fastify/adapters/fastify-adapter.ts, dd90f57f
#
#     this.instance.use(normalizedPath, (req: any, res: any, next: Function) => {
#       const queryParamsIndex = req.originalUrl.indexOf('?');
#       let pathname =
#         queryParamsIndex >= 0
#           ? req.originalUrl.slice(0, queryParamsIndex)
#           : req.originalUrl;
#       pathname = safeDecodeURI(pathname).path;
#
#       if (!re.exec(pathname + '/') && normalizedPath) {
#         return next();                      // <- middleware skipped
#       }
#       return callback(req, res, next);
#     });
#
#   packages/platform-fastify/adapters/middie/fastify-middie.ts, dd90f57f
#
#     if (regexp) {
#       // Decode URL before matching to avoid bypassing middleware
#       const decodedUrl = safeDecodeURI(url).path;
#       const result = regexp.exec(decodedUrl);
#       if (result) { ... fn(req, res, that.done); } else { that.done(); }
#     }
#
# `re`/`regexp` is `pathToRegexp` of the path the application registered with
# `MiddlewareConsumer.forRoutes()`. So for an application configured with any of
# the four router options, `GET /abc/def/`, `/abc//def`, `/ABC/DEF` or
# `/abc/def;x=1` is routed by find-my-way to the handler registered at
# `/abc/def` — the router normalized it — while the middleware regexp is tested
# against the un-normalized spelling and declines. The middleware does not
# error; it is skipped, and the handler answers unguarded. Where the middleware
# is the authentication, authorization, CSRF or rate-limiting layer, that is a
# complete bypass of it for every route it covers.
#
# Who the attacker is matters here, because Nest is a framework. This is not a
# weakness in code written against Nest and it is not something an application
# can be careless about: the matching is Nest's own, inside the platform
# adapter, and the guarantee it breaks is the framework's — that middleware
# registered `forRoutes(X)` runs for every request the router dispatches to X.
# The attacker is any client that can reach the route, with no credential and
# no application bug required.
#
# The fixed revision (d74e9a8c, merged as fd8d073e) makes both matchers read the four
# options off `initialConfig`/`initialConfig.routerOptions` and apply them
# before decoding, and passes `useSemicolonDelimiter` into `safeDecodeURI`'s
# documented second parameter. The adapter does it in a new private
# `sanitizeUrl()`; middie does it inline in the matching closure, threading
# `fastify.initialConfig` through `middie(onMiddieEnd, initialConfig)`.
#
# Pool row: correct. `fd8d073e` is the merge of PR #16384 and its combined diff
# is empty, but `git diff dd90f57f fd8d073e` reads the whole patch, and the
# single commit inside it, d74e9a8c, has the same three-file stat. `dd90f57f`
# is the fully vulnerable tree and the remediation is present at `fd8d073e`.
# Recorded CWE is a hint worth restating: the interpretation conflict between
# router and matcher is CWE-436, and the effect is a control that never runs,
# CWE-863.
#
# FIXED. Diagnosis first. There is no taint path to want: the defect is which
# string a regular expression is tested against, not a value reaching a sink.
# A bounded scan of packages/platform-fastify at dd90f57f reported two findings
# and neither is this CVE. One is VYQL-AUTH-119, rank 1388's binding, on
# fastify-middie.ts:209 — a true positive for a *different*, still-unfixed
# weakness, because Nest's vendored middie clone re-registers inherited
# middleware through `(instance.use as any)(...middleware)`. The other is
# VYQL-INJ-002 at fastify-adapter.ts:709 -> 713, which lands on the vulnerable
# line for the wrong reason: it is the known defect that labels a regular
# expression's `.exec()` as code.CommandExecution. Both findings are present
# unchanged at fd8d073e, so the pre-existing state gave 174 findings on the
# whole monorepo at each revision — no discrimination at all.
#
# New concept code.MiddlewarePathMatchNormalizationMismatch with its review
# twin and VYQL-AUTH-127 (minted above the campaign's reported AUTH ceiling of
# 126; this worktree's pack maximum is 122). Reuse is considered and did not
# fit: code.PathMatcherNormalizationOrderBypass is about a matcher that trims
# platform-ignored suffixes *before* canonical cleaning and is scoped to
# redirect and traversal outcomes, whereas this matcher's ordering is fine and
# its canonicalization is simply narrower than the dispatcher's;
# code.EncapsulatedMiddlewarePathReprefixed is the same effect (middleware
# mounted where it cannot match) from an unrelated cause, prefix arithmetic on
# registration rather than path spelling at request time;
# code.UrlPathParameterCaseNormalizationBypass covers parameter *names* stored
# under caller-controlled case, not the route path.
#
# The binding is javascript/find_my_way_path_match_normalization.vyql, a
# function-scope presence predicate keyed on find-my-way's API and on nothing
# about Nest: `call_path:safeDecodeURI` (find-my-way/lib/url-sanitizer, the
# percent-decoding step alone), `call:exec` or `call:test`, a `req.url` /
# `req.originalUrl` selector, and no read of `ignoreDuplicateSlashes`,
# `ignoreTrailingSlash`, `caseSensitive` or `useSemicolonDelimiter`. Someone who
# has never seen this CVE can write it from find-my-way's option documentation
# plus the signature of its own sanitizer.
#
# Three robustness checks, all on real files rather than the snippets below.
#
#   Renaming the fixed revision's own vocabulary does not bring the flag back. Rewriting
#   `sanitizeUrl`, `removeDuplicateSlashes`, `trimLastSlash` and `bindLast` to
#   meaningless names throughout the patched pair leaves 0 flags, because the
#   discriminator is the router's option names and not the fixed revision'six's helpers.
#
#   Comments cannot silence it. Inserting `// ignoreTrailingSlash,
#   ignoreDuplicateSlashes, caseSensitive and useSemicolonDelimiter are handled
#   by the router.` immediately above each vulnerable `safeDecodeURI`
#   call leaves the flags at 6, because `selector:` tokens are member accesses
#   derived from the AST.
#
#   A remediation written a different way is also accepted. Normalizing inline
#   in the two vulnerable functions — no helper at all, and the config read from
#   a differently named property (`that.cfg`) — gives 0 flags, so the decline is
#   attributable to the option reads rather than to the shape the fixed revision chooses.
#
# Measured (timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB, --cache off; counts are a union over
# three runs and every one of these is stable across the three). The whole nest
# monorepo scans in 22s at 1712 JavaScript/TypeScript files, so this is a
# whole-tree measurement rather than a subtree one.
#
#   nest whole tree: base 174 findings at dd90f57f and 174 at fd8d073e; with
#   this binding 180 and 174. The delta is exactly six VYQL-AUTH-127 rows on the
#   vulnerable revision, at fastify-adapter.ts:672/678/705 and
#   fastify-middie.ts:36/107/116. Six rows for two defects is the nested-context
#   inheritance cost noted in the binding: 705 and 116 are the matching closures
#   and the other four are the factory functions enclosing them. It is not
#   suppressible from data, since an enclosing context is a superset of the
#   inner one's tokens.
#
# Collateral, enumerated rather than sampled. `call_path:safeDecodeURI` is the
# narrowest conjunct, so the reachable set is every JavaScript or TypeScript
# file mentioning `safeDecodeURI` across the 160 clone directories on this
# machine. There are three sources outside this rank's own probe copies:
# nest's platform-fastify (the target), rank 1388's three derived copies of
# nest's fastify-middie.ts, and rank 1346's vendored angular.js — where the
# name is `safeDecodeURIComponent`, declined by the exact `==`, confirmed by
# scanning that file alone for 0 flags. Scanned whole: rank1325 (a *later*,
# patched nest checkout, 2160 findings) 0 flags, and rank1412,
# rank1403, rank1381 and rank1388 0 flags each.
#
# Neither OWASP corpus can move: the binding is JavaScript-only and the new rule
# consumes only the new concept, which nothing else emits, while both corpora
# are Java and Python.

test "rank1429: a Fastify middleware matcher percent-decodes the request path and matches it without the router's slash, case and delimiter normalization"
  lang javascript
  file fastify-adapter.ts
  expect VYQL-AUTH-127
  expect_review code.MiddlewarePathMatchNormalizationMismatch
  code
  ```
  import { safeDecodeURI } from 'find-my-way/lib/url-sanitizer';
  import { pathToRegexp } from 'path-to-regexp';

  export class FastifyAdapter {
    public createMiddlewareFactory(requestMethod: number) {
      return (path: string, callback: Function) => {
        const normalizedPath = path.endsWith('/*') ? `${path.slice(0, -1)}(.*)` : path;
        const { regexp: re } = pathToRegexp(normalizedPath);

        this.instance.use(
          normalizedPath,
          (req: any, res: any, next: Function) => {
            const queryParamsIndex = req.originalUrl.indexOf('?');
            let pathname =
              queryParamsIndex >= 0
                ? req.originalUrl.slice(0, queryParamsIndex)
                : req.originalUrl;
            pathname = safeDecodeURI(pathname).path;

            if (!re.exec(pathname + '/') && normalizedPath) {
              return next();
            }
            return callback(req, res, next);
          },
        );
      };
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1429: a connect-style middleware engine tests the registered route's regular expression against a path it only percent-decoded"
  lang javascript
  file fastify-middie.ts
  expect VYQL-AUTH-127
  expect_review code.MiddlewarePathMatchNormalizationMismatch
  code
  ```
  import { safeDecodeURI } from 'find-my-way/lib/url-sanitizer';

  function middie(complete) {
    const middlewares = [];

    function runMiddleware(that) {
      const req = that.req;
      const res = that.res;
      const url = that.url;
      const i = that.i++;

      req.url = req.originalUrl;

      const { fn, regexp } = middlewares[i];
      if (regexp) {
        const decodedUrl = safeDecodeURI(url).path;
        const result = regexp.exec(decodedUrl);
        if (result) {
          req.url = req.url.replace(result[0], '');
          if (req.url[0] !== '/') req.url = '/' + req.url;
          fn(req, res, that.done);
        } else {
          that.done();
        }
      } else {
        fn(req, res, that.done);
      }
    }

    return { use, run: runMiddleware };
  }
  ```

test "rank1429: the matcher reads the router's normalization options before decoding"
  lang javascript
  file fastify-middie.ts
  reject VYQL-AUTH-127
  code
  ```
  import { safeDecodeURI } from 'find-my-way/lib/url-sanitizer';

  function middie(complete, initialConfig) {
    const middlewares = [];

    function runMiddleware(that) {
      const req = that.req;
      const res = that.res;
      const url = that.url;
      const i = that.i++;

      req.url = req.originalUrl;

      const { fn, regexp } = middlewares[i];
      if (regexp) {
        let sanitizedUrl = url;
        if (
          that.initialConfig.ignoreDuplicateSlashes ||
          that.initialConfig.routerOptions?.ignoreDuplicateSlashes
        ) {
          sanitizedUrl = removeDuplicateSlashes(sanitizedUrl);
        }

        if (
          that.initialConfig.ignoreTrailingSlash ||
          that.initialConfig.routerOptions?.ignoreTrailingSlash
        ) {
          sanitizedUrl = trimLastSlash(sanitizedUrl);
        }

        if (
          that.initialConfig.caseSensitive === false ||
          that.initialConfig.routerOptions?.caseSensitive === false
        ) {
          sanitizedUrl = sanitizedUrl.toLowerCase();
        }

        const decodedUrl = safeDecodeURI(
          sanitizedUrl,
          that.initialConfig.routerOptions?.useSemicolonDelimiter ||
            that.initialConfig.useSemicolonDelimiter,
        ).path;
        const result = regexp.exec(decodedUrl);
        if (result) {
          req.url = req.url.replace(result[0], '');
          if (req.url[0] !== '/') req.url = '/' + req.url;
          fn(req, res, that.done);
        } else {
          that.done();
        }
      } else {
        fn(req, res, that.done);
      }
    }

    return { use, run: bindLast(runMiddleware, initialConfig) };
  }
  ```

test "rank1429: the adapter canonicalizes the path through a helper that applies the router's options, so the matching closure does not decode for itself"
  lang javascript
  file fastify-adapter.ts
  reject VYQL-AUTH-127
  code
  ```
  import { safeDecodeURI } from 'find-my-way/lib/url-sanitizer';
  import { pathToRegexp } from 'path-to-regexp';

  export class FastifyAdapter {
    public createMiddlewareFactory(requestMethod: number) {
      return (path: string, callback: Function) => {
        const normalizedPath = path.endsWith('/*') ? `${path.slice(0, -1)}(.*)` : path;
        const { regexp: re } = pathToRegexp(normalizedPath);

        this.instance.use(
          normalizedPath,
          (req: any, res: any, next: Function) => {
            const queryParamsIndex = req.originalUrl.indexOf('?');
            let pathname =
              queryParamsIndex >= 0
                ? req.originalUrl.slice(0, queryParamsIndex)
                : req.originalUrl;

            pathname = this.sanitizeUrl(pathname);

            if (!re.exec(pathname + '/') && normalizedPath) {
              return next();
            }
            return callback(req, res, next);
          },
        );
      };
    }

    private sanitizeUrl(url: string): string {
      const initialConfig = this.instance.initialConfig;
      const routerOptions = initialConfig.routerOptions;

      if (
        routerOptions.ignoreDuplicateSlashes ||
        initialConfig.ignoreDuplicateSlashes
      ) {
        url = this.removeDuplicateSlashes(url);
      }

      if (routerOptions.ignoreTrailingSlash || initialConfig.ignoreTrailingSlash) {
        url = this.trimLastSlash(url);
      }

      if (
        routerOptions.caseSensitive === false ||
        initialConfig.caseSensitive === false
      ) {
        url = url.toLowerCase();
      }
      return safeDecodeURI(
        url,
        routerOptions.useSemicolonDelimiter || initialConfig.useSemicolonDelimiter,
      ).path;
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1429: a URL parser that decodes and matches without deciding middleware dispatch is not this weakness"
  lang javascript
  file cookies.js
  reject VYQL-AUTH-127
  code
  ```
  import { safeDecodeURI } from 'find-my-way/lib/url-sanitizer';

  const PAIR = /([^=]+)=(.*)/;

  export function parseCookies(header) {
    const out = {};
    for (const cookie of header.split(';')) {
      const match = PAIR.exec(cookie.trim());
      if (match) {
        out[safeDecodeURI(match[1]).path] = safeDecodeURI(match[2]).path;
      }
    }
    return out;
  }
  ```
